From: =?UTF-8?q?Christian=20F=C3=BCrst?= <christian.fuerst@ludofact.de>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:59:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix: ask Azure DevOps for a 401 instead of its sign-in page

Azure DevOps answers a rejected token, whether invalid, expired or issued
for another audience, with a 302 to its sign-in page unless the request
sends X-TFS-FedAuthRedirect: Suppress. azure-devops-node-api always sends
it. The tools' own fetch calls do not, and fetch follows the redirect:
search_wiki and search_workitem then return the sign-in page as a
successful result, and tools that parse JSON fail with "Unexpected token
'<'".

The HTTP entry point now adds the header to every fetch, so those tools
report a 401 with Azure DevOps' own message. The wrapper changes no
credential and keeps no state. The stdio entry point has the same problem;
drop this patch once upstream fixes it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/http.ts | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/http.ts b/src/http.ts
index 8a5bcbe..18d2944 100644
--- a/src/http.ts
+++ b/src/http.ts
@@ -74,6 +74,16 @@ const organization = argv.organization as string;
 setOrgName(organization);
 const enabledDomains = new DomainsManager(argv.domains).getEnabledDomains();
 
+// Azure DevOps answers a rejected token with a 302 to its sign-in page unless a request asks it not to, and fetch
+// follows the redirect, so a tool would read the sign-in page as its result. azure-devops-node-api already sends
+// this header; this adds it to the tools' own fetch calls. It changes no credential.
+const fetchFollowingSignInRedirects = globalThis.fetch;
+globalThis.fetch = (input, init) => {
+  const headers = new Headers(init?.headers ?? (input instanceof Request ? input.headers : undefined));
+  headers.set("X-TFS-FedAuthRedirect", "Suppress");
+  return fetchFollowingSignInRedirects(input, { ...init, headers });
+};
+
 const server = createHttpServer({ organization, enabledDomains, path: argv.path });
 
 server.listen(argv.port, argv.host, () => {
